  "user_scenario": "You are Renata Ellery, forty, a middle-school librarian in Somerville, Massachusetts. You call Halverson Clinical Laboratories Patient Services on a Tuesday morning, calm and a little tired, because you have made up your mind: you want to cancel the genetic test your doctor at Fresh Pond Family Medicine ordered for you in September, the hereditary cancer panel. You open with exactly that - you would like to cancel the genetic test, the hereditary cancer panel - and you say you have thought about it for a few weeks and do not want it run right now. You never signed the consent form for it and you are not going to. You do not know internal codes, order numbers or how fees work, and you do not volunteer anything about money.\nWhen the agent asks who you are, you give your phone number, 6174419027, and, if a second detail is asked for, your date of birth, 1986-05-14. Your email is renata.ellery@fastmail.com and your ZIP code is 02144, but you only give those if the agent specifically asks for them. If the agent asks which test, it is the genetic one - the hereditary cancer panel (they also added a cystic fibrosis carrier screen to it, you believe); the blood draws were done at your home by Halverson's mobile service. If asked about insurance, you have Harborline Health PPO through your employer, and you remember that the plan turned down the approval for the genetic test, after which you told Halverson you would pay for it yourself. If asked about financial help, you were approved for Halverson's financial assistance over the summer. If asked why you are cancelling, you simply say you are not ready to have it done.\nYou let the agent do whatever they say has to be handled on their side before the cancellation, and you never bring up any other test or any other item yourself. If the agent tells you something has to be recorded or issued on your account first, you say that is fine and to go ahead. If the agent tells you that other blood work of yours needs to be drawn again and gives you a date by which it must be done, you thank them and say you will set up the home visit yourself through the patient portal; you do not ask them to book it. If the agent tells you there is a cancellation fee, you accept it without arguing and without guessing what it should be; if they tell you the amount, you note it. Once the agent explains that you have to confirm the cancellation yourself and hands you the cancellation step, you run it for the genetic test order with the order reference and the fee amount exactly as the agent gives them to you. When you see that the order now shows as cancelled, you thank the agent and end the call. If the agent tells you the test cannot be cancelled, you say you understand and end the call.",
